\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Formal interaction under Gaussian elimination}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{Eliminating a field with a quartic interaction generally creates
quadratic and sextic terms. The coefficients through second order
can be found by conditional Gaussian moments, while preserving a
supplied symmetry and composition of eliminations.}

\p{Use the finite orthogonal sum #{E=X\mathbin\perp Y}, positive
precision #{Q}, components #{A,B,C}, Schur precision #{S} and scalar
#{c_C} of [Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D). Let
#{T:E^4\to\mathbb R} be a symmetric four-linear form and put
##{V(u)=\frac{T(u,u,u,u)}{4!}.}
Write #{\mathcal P(X)} for the [polynomial function algebra](fdip-002V).
For #{x\in X}, let #{\eta} have centered Gaussian law with covariance
#{C^{-1}} on #{Y}, and set
##{v_x=(x,-C^{-1}B^\dagger x+\eta).}
This is the conditional law of the eliminated field given the retained
value. It is the [conditional kernel](fdip-003A) with its two named
summands exchanged; its covariance is #{C^{-1}}, whereas that
proposition's retained-given-eliminated kernel has covariance #{A^{-1}}.}

\p{For a formal variable #{\lambda}, use ordinary power-series
coefficients in #{\lambda} and define the
\newvocab{fiber effective interaction} by
##{\begin{aligned}
F_\lambda(x)&=-\log\mathbb E_\eta e^{-\lambda V(v_x)},\\
\mathbb E_\eta e^{-\lambda V(v_x)}
 &=\sum_{n\geq0}\frac{(-\lambda)^n}{n!}
       \mathbb E_\eta V(v_x)^n.
\end{aligned}}
These are formal series in #{\lambda} with coefficients in
#{\mathcal P(X)}; the logarithm
is the formal logarithm of a series with constant coefficient one.
Every coefficient is finite by [polynomial integrability](fdip-002W).
The notation #{O(\lambda^3)} means a series divisible by #{\lambda^3},
not an analytic error estimate. With
##{\begin{aligned}
a(x)&=\mathbb E[V\mid x],\\
b(x)&=\mathbb E[V^2\mid x]-a(x)^2,
\end{aligned}}
the first two coefficients are
##{F_\lambda=\lambda a-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}b+O(\lambda^3).}
The degree-zero terms are retained: coefficientwise integration gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_Y e^{-\frac12\langle(x,y),Q(x,y)\rangle}
                    e^{-\lambda V(x,y)}\,dy\\
&\qquad=c_Ce^{-\frac12\langle x,Sx\rangle}e^{-F_\lambda(x)}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{dy} is the specified Euclidean volume. This fiber logarithm
integrates eliminated variables; it does not take the Legendre
transform of a source-generating function used for a one-particle
irreducible (1PI) effective action in
\citet{v2, Proposition 3.23, p. 50}{etingof2024mathematical}.}

\p{For a specified orthogonal flag
#{E=X\mathbin\perp Y\mathbin\perp Z}, first eliminate #{Z} and then
#{Y}. All conditional expectations below use the original Gaussian
law, with the #{Y}-given-#{X} kernel obtained after eliminating #{Z}.
Put
##{\begin{aligned}
a_1(x,y)&=\mathbb E[V\mid x,y],\\
b_1(x,y)&=\mathbb E[V^2\mid x,y]-a_1(x,y)^2.
\end{aligned}}
Both stages together give
##{\begin{aligned}
F_\lambda(x)
={}&\lambda\mathbb E[a_1\mid x]\\
&-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\bigl(
 \mathbb E[b_1\mid x]+\operatorname{Var}(a_1\mid x)\bigr)\\
 &+O(\lambda^3),
\end{aligned}}
which agrees with direct elimination of #{Y\oplus Z}. The first
coefficient has degrees among #{4,2,0}; the second has degrees among
#{6,4,2,0}. Particular components may vanish.}

\proof{
\p{Expand the expectation as
#{1-\lambda a+\lambda^2\mathbb E[V^2\mid x]/2+O(\lambda^3)}.
The identity #{\log(1+u)=u-u^2/2+O(u^3)} gives the displayed
coefficient #{-b/2}. Square completion in \ref{fdip-000D} identifies
the normalized conditional Gaussian and the raw scalar #{c_C};
applying it to each polynomial coefficient proves the fiber identity.
No convergence or interchange of an infinite series and an integral
is needed.}
\p{The first-stage interaction is
#{F_1=\lambda a_1-\lambda^2b_1/2+O(\lambda^3)}. Hence
##{e^{-F_1}=1-\lambda a_1+
 \frac{\lambda^2}{2}(a_1^2+b_1)+O(\lambda^3).}
Take the #{Y}-given-#{X} expectation and the negative logarithm.
This yields the stated two-stage formula. Polynomial integrability
permits signed Fubini for every coefficient, so the tower identities
for these Gaussian kernels give
##{\begin{aligned}
&\mathbb E[a_1\mid x]=\mathbb E[V\mid x],\\
&\mathbb E[b_1\mid x]+\operatorname{Var}(a_1\mid x)\\
&\qquad=\mathbb E[V^2\mid x]-\mathbb E[V\mid x]^2.
\end{aligned}}
These identities also follow directly by expanding #{b_1} and
cancelling #{\mathbb E[a_1^2\mid x]}. They prove composition
through the stated order without discarding any generated term.}
\p{The mean of #{v_x} is linear in #{x}, and its centered noise is
independent of #{x}. Expanding the four-linear tensor and pairing
centered Gaussian factors by Wick's theorem leaves degrees #{4,2,0}
in #{a}. In #{\mathbb E[V^2\mid x]-\mathbb E[V\mid x]^2}, pairings
with no contraction between the two copies of #{V} cancel. Every
remaining pairing uses at least two noise factors. The eight original
factors therefore leave at most six mean factors. An even number of
noise factors is paired, giving only degrees #{6,4,2,0}. The
homogeneous components are symmetric tensors on #{X} by polarization;
they need not be diagonal in a chosen coordinate basis.}
}

\p{Suppose a second such model is related by a linear isometric isomorphism
#{L=L_X\oplus L_Y:E\to E'} satisfying
#{Q'L=LQ} and #{V'\circ L=V}. Then
##{F'_\lambda(L_Xx)=F_\lambda(x)\pmod{\lambda^3}.}
Indeed the component identities give
#{C'L_Y=L_YC} and #{(B')^\dagger L_X=L_YB^\dagger}.
Thus #{L_Y} transports the conditional mean and covariance, hence
its Gaussian law and both moments #{a,b}. For two-stage elimination
also require preservation of the supplied flag.
In particular, a supplied finite group of these isometries preserves
each induced coefficient. The result transports a given action;
it does not select that action.}

\p{Wick contractions are
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical}.
The perturbation convention in
\citet{v2, Section 3.3, p. 33}{etingof2024mathematical}
is #{B(u,u)/2-\sum_i g_iB_i(u,\ldots,u)/i!}; here
#{g_4=-\lambda} and #{B_4=T}, at #{\hbar=1}.
The conditional formulas and composition above are direct deductions
using these contractions and the specified Gaussian kernels. The
[three-coordinate example](fdip-0047) exhibits every indicated degree.}
